=== REASONING ===

ADR-015 invariants this slice touches and how the design satisfies each:

  I1  default-off               Registry entry DefaultOn:false; no startup auto-enable.
  I3  baseline intact           The deterministic speed-profile chart + summary metrics are
                                untouched. The new <SectionErrorBoundary> wrapping the AI
                                section is in addition to — not in place of — the chart's
                                existing SectionErrorBoundary. /api/v1/drives/{id}/speed-profile
                                handler and its SQL are not modified.
  I4  zero egress               Strategy package has no provider import; the provider client is
                                only resolved by the dispatcher at request time, which the
                                guard short-circuits in off mode.
  I5  hidden UI                 withAiFeature('speed-profile-insights', InnerSection) returns
                                null in off mode AND when the toggle is false. The wrapping div
                                with data-testid is only emitted by withAiFeature when the gate
                                is open. Off-mode test asserts the testid is absent.
  I6  404 routes                The route is mounted exclusively inside r.Route("/ai", …) with
                                g.Wrap("speed-profile-insights", …) — guard returns 404 when
                                ai_mode='off' OR the per-feature toggle is false (P6).
  I7  per-feature               Registry entry has DefaultOn:false. Backend guard checks the
                                per-feature toggle; HOC checks it on the client. Off-mode test
                                exercises mode='off' WITH the per-feature toggle true to defeat
                                the trivial "off because everything is off" pass.
  I10 type system               aigen regenerated web/src/ai/features.ts; aivet confirms registry
                                ↔ TS mirror are in sync; tsc --noEmit is clean.
  I12 client/bg                 No new push kinds; no new background jobs; no new client-storage
                                keys; one new lazy chunk surface (ai-speed-profile-insights) is
                                already provided by the existing React.lazy split inside the
                                drive-detail bundle (no top-level route added). Registry
                                explicitly lists JobNames=[] and PushKinds=[] so the final gate
                                proves absence.

P-rule compliance:

  P1 provider port              All LLM calls go through the dispatcher → registry → provider
                                pattern already wired by F1/F4. Strategy package does not import
                                a provider adapter directly (verified by grep:
                                strategies/speed-profile-insights/*.go imports nothing from
                                /provider/* — only /strategy, /provider/types, /tools/types).
  P4 strategy/dispatcher loop   AISpeedProfileInsightsHandler.ServeHTTP delegates to
                                dispatch.Run with the strategy + tool registry + denyAllConfirm
                                — same as drive-coaching.
  P5 decorator chain            Provider resolution honours the F1 decorator order baked into
                                the registry (redaction → rate-limit → cost-cap → audit → trace).
                                This slice adds no new decorators.
  P6 guard                      ai.GuardedHandler is the only mount path; both backend and
                                frontend gates use the same feature id "speed-profile-insights".
  P8 evals                      goldens.yaml has 3 deterministic cases (city_drive,
                                highway_drive, refusal_other_drive). Canned files under
                                strategies/speed-profile-insights/canned/* supply the mock
                                provider replies for each.
  P9 registry                   Registry entry lists Backend/Frontend/UITestIDs explicitly +
                                explicit empty JobNames/PushKinds so coverage proves absence.
  P10 baseline coexistence      SpeedProfileInsightProvider selection is purely at the React
                                composition layer (HOC) and at the backend route layer (guard).
                                No business-logic branch in baseline code.

Tool design choices:

  query_speed_profile           Returns SI aggregates from *models.Drive — distance_m, duration_s,
                                avg/max speed mps, kWh, derived avg/max kmh + a single
                                speed_regime string from the same SQL CASE thresholds as
                                /api/v1/drives/{id}/speed-profile. NO new SQL — reads
                                DriveRepo.GetByID, derives in-memory.
  query_drive_context           Returns temporal + battery + ambient-temp envelope from the
                                same row (started_at, ended_at, ended_status, start/end
                                battery pct, outside temp avg c, distance_m, duration_s).
                                Intentionally EXCLUDES start_lat/start_lon/end_lat/end_lon and
                                start_address/end_address — only presence-flags are returned
                                (has_start_address, has_end_address, has_route_coordinates).
                                The test TestBuildDriveContext_ExcludesPreciseRouteData pins
                                this privacy contract.

Redaction policy choice:

  PolicySpeedProfileInsights    Allow=[ClassVehicleName], Mode=ModeRedactedTags. The strategy
                                only sees aggregates by design; the redactor is the second
                                belt — even if a future tool ever yielded a raw address string,
                                ClassVehicleName-only allows the vehicle nickname through and
                                redacts every other PII class (Person, Phone, Email, Address,
                                Lat/Lon, VIN, etc.).

Frontend route registration:

  Registry Frontend value:      ["/drives/:id"]
                                The SPA route key is :id (per AppRouter.tsx and DriveDetailPage's
                                useParams<{id:string}>()). The chi URL parameter on the backend
                                is {driveID} (case-sensitive). The two systems do not need to
                                share the parameter name — they only need to share the feature
                                id used by the guard + the HOC.

i18n strategy:

  web/src/i18n/en.json  — added settings.ai.feature["speed-profile-insights"] (label + description),
                          driveDetail.aiSpeedProfile (title, description, badge, generateButton),
                          driveDetail.section.aiSpeedProfileInsightsFailed.
  web/src/i18n/ar.json,
  web/src/i18n/he.json  — placeholder-only locale files (status:"placeholder" in _meta).
                          Per the established N-tier slice pattern (slices 0011–0021 left
                          ar.json/he.json untouched for every AI-only key), i18next's
                          configured fallbackLng="en" satisfies these locales without any
                          edit. Documented here for the honesty covenant — this is not a
                          missed translation, it is the project-wide convention.

Verification command substitution:

  The prompt's "make ai-eval-fast --feature speed-profile-insights" target does not exist in
  the project Makefile (the same was true for slices 0018–0021). Per the precedent in
  phase-50-0021-nl-drive-search-replay.log §GATE L263, the run is performed via the underlying
  binary: `go run ./cmd/ai-eval -feature speed-profile-insights`. This is the same eval
  harness the 9999 final gate invokes.

